          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does adherence mean in the context of diabetes treatment, and why does it matter?",
          "answer": "Adherence means how closely a person follows the treatment plan they agreed with their clinician. For diabetes, that includes taking glucose-lowering medication as prescribed, checking blood glucose when advised, attending reviews, and following the eating and activity guidance. The guidance notes that diabetes is largely self-managed, since most of the decisions that affect the outcome are made by the person between appointments (Sticking with a diabetes regimen: monitoring, medication and review).\n\nThe same source says that adherence to glucose-lowering medication is measurably imperfect in practice. It also says that missed doses are not simply a matter of character. Cost, complicated dosing schedules, side effects, low health literacy, depression, and simply forgetting account for most of it, and each has a different solution. The guidance suggests that clinicians look for the specific cause rather than assume the person is unwilling.\n\nThe corpus does not give specific figures on how much poor adherence changes long-term outcomes in diabetes, so I cannot quantify that here. It does say that structured diabetes self-management education improves glucose control and quality of life, and that it is often underused. It is offered at diagnosis, yearly when targets are not met, when complications develop, and during changes in care.\n\nIf you or someone you know is finding it hard to keep to a diabetes plan, a useful first step is to tell the treating clinician or diabetes team the specific barrier, such as cost, side effects, or a schedule that is hard to keep. They can then look at what might help. Do not stop or change any medication without speaking to a clinician first.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
